body:
Hi, I've investigated the issue. Can you replicate this on other compositors or the compatibility renderer?

I can't seem to replicate this bug outside of kwin using the Forward+ and Mobile renderers. Any other compositor seem to work and the Compatibility renderer works everywhere including kwin. Interestingly, in the cases the embedded game does not get stopped, clicking the "Game" tab shows the game for a split second and then it closes by its own.

I also did some probing with GDB and all roads are leading to FIFO. The details are complicated but, in short: historically the renderer would freeze when the window has occluded and the only way to work around it was to throttle frames manually. This has been solved with the new `fifo-v1` protocol. When `fifo-v1` is available, we blindly trust the compositor. Apparently in this case the game is locking during rendering for the exact same reason despite using FIFO.

This is further confirmed by this patch, which seems to work around the issue for me:
```diff
--- a/platform/linuxbsd/wayland/wayland_thread.cpp
+++ b/platform/linuxbsd/wayland/wayland_thread.cpp
@@ -6139,7 +6139,7 @@ bool WaylandThread::window_is_suspended(DisplayServerEnums::WindowID p_window_id
 }

 bool WaylandThread::is_fifo_available() const {
-       return registry.wp_fifo_manager_name != 0;
+       return false;
 }

 bool WaylandThread::is_suspended() const {
```

(It forces the frame throttling hack)

This can't be made default without worsening performance for working implementations (like mutter) but perhaps we could add an environment variable to force it?

There have been some recent commits in kwin for a similar issue such as [this commit](https://invent.kde.org/plasma/kwin/-/commit/de7abf22cb73b2faa1a598de37deacd8457552a0) but unfortunately I can still replicate this bug on the latest kwin bleeding edge build on KDE Linux `2026-05-24`.

I've reported this issue to kwin developer @Zamundaaa and hopefully they'll be able to give us more information and confirm whether or not the bug is due to Godot's Wayland backend or due to kwin.
